today Londoners have woken up to a very  different city with up to 40 tube  stations closed and bus and train  services cut as coronavirus takes hold  here more quickly than anywhere else in  the UK the mayor Sadiq Khan has called  for people to stop using the transport  network unless it is essential now our  ministers and officials are considering  plans to go further they could restrict  use of public transport for everyone  except key workers and close most shops  here to curb the spread of the virus  experts are worried that intensive care  units in London could become overwhelmed  within two to three weeks one hospital  in the south of the city has nearly  reached capacity in its ICU already  because London is ahead of the rest of  the country by a couple of weeks or so  then I think there is a there is an  argument for putting measures in place  in London more rapidly than elsewhere  but to be putting in putting the same  measures in place elsewhere will still  be beneficial elsewhere and will still  save cases elsewhere so if you're gonna  do it this a strong argument for doing  it everywhere at the same time Boris  Johnson refused to rule out tougher  measures yesterday and the government's  chief scientific adviser said people  must take instructions seriously the  moment we say well it's not for me it's  for somebody else and the compliance of  this goes down the less effective is  going to be so when we don't adhere to  this we're actually putting lots of  people at risk we're putting those  sickest patients at risk and so it's  crucial that this isn't taken as soft  advice ministers have fire to do  whatever it takes to beat this virus the  reality of that message may be far  tougher than any expected Kate McCann  Sky News
 

